# Expectation record for tests/conformance/03_initializers/001_scalar_and_array.c
# Area 03 baseline: scalar and array initialization, every element read back individually.

program            = 001_scalar_and_array
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Baseline scalar and array initialization for static and automatic storage duration, with every array element and every struct member read back and printed individually
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour.

Every object is fully initialized by its initializer before any read: g_scalar,
g_arr, g_u_arr and g_pt have static storage duration with explicit initializers;
l_scalar, l_arr and l_pt are automatic with explicit initializers. There is no
read of uninitialized storage anywhere.

No arithmetic is performed on any initialized value, so no signed overflow is
possible. The only arithmetic is the loop counter i, which runs 0..4 and 0..2 over
arrays of 5 and 3 elements respectively, so every subscript is strictly in bounds
and no one-past-end pointer is ever formed, let alone dereferenced.

No shifts. No pointer casts, and no pointer value printed, compared or converted to an
integer: the pointer arithmetic the language performs for a subscript, and the decay of
each format-string literal at its printf call, are the only pointer operations present. No
aliasing: each object is read only through its own declared type. No object is modified twice
between sequence points
initialized test data -- g_scalar, g_arr, g_u_arr, g_pt, l_scalar, l_arr and
l_pt -- is never modified after its initializer at all, so every value the
program prints is the value its initializer gave it. The one object that is
modified is the loop counter i: it is assigned in the initial clause of each of
the three for statements at 001_scalar_and_array.c lines 43, 46 and 53 and
incremented in each of their controlling expressions, and every one of those
assignments and increments is a full expression of its own, so each is separated
from every other access to i by a sequence point. Its range is bounded by a
literal in each case -- i < 5, i < 3 and i < 5 over arrays of 5, 3 and 5
elements -- so it neither overflows nor leaves an array. Each printf call takes
at most one argument with a side effect (none do). No padding byte is read or
printed: only named members g_pt.x, g_pt.y, l_pt.x, l_pt.y are accessed, never
the struct as a whole and never its representation.

4294967295u is exactly UINT_MAX for a 32-bit unsigned int and is written with the
u suffix, so it is representable without conversion on every target and no
implementation-defined narrowing occurs.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
All values are int or unsigned int, which are 4 bytes on all four supported
targets, and are printed with %d and %u only. The program contains no long,
size_t, ptrdiff_t, intptr_t, float, double or long double, so the measured width
differences between i686 (4-byte long and pointer) and the other three targets
(8-byte) cannot affect the output.

The only character data is the format-string literals, whose bytes printf copies
through unchanged. No plain-char value is read as a number, converted, compared or
formatted numerically, so the measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on
x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) cannot arise.

No address or pointer value is printed. No struct is printed as a whole, so
padding bytes -- whose contents are unspecified -- are never observed.

Output is therefore byte-identical on all four targets at all three optimization
levels, which was verified by measurement.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
g_scalar=42
g_arr[0]=10
g_arr[1]=20
g_arr[2]=30
g_arr[3]=40
g_arr[4]=50
g_u_arr[0]=0
g_u_arr[1]=1
g_u_arr[2]=4294967295
g_pt.x=7
g_pt.y=-9
l_scalar=42
l_arr[0]=10
l_arr[1]=20
l_arr[2]=30
l_arr[3]=40
l_arr[4]=50
l_pt.x=7
l_pt.y=-9
END
